(window.GROUP_QUIZZES=window.GROUP_QUIZZES||{})["clinical-medicine-and-surgery-i-exam-2-ocular-trauma-quiz-version-2"]={"title":"Ocular Trauma Quiz 2 — CMS I Exam 2","category":"Clinical Medicine and Surgery I","exam":"Exam 2","sem":"fall-2026","questions":[{"q":"What position is the patient placed in while awaiting repair of a retinal detachment?","choices":["Lying on the affected side","Head of bed elevated 30 to 40 degrees","Flat, with the head lower than the body","Prone with the face down","Sitting fully upright"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct, alongside pain control and antiemetics. — CMS I Ocular Trauma - Shah Fallsv.pptx, Slide 35"},{"q":"An open globe injury is suspected. What is placed over the eye?","choices":["A pressure patch","A saline-soaked gauze dressing","Nothing, so the eye can be re-examined","An ice pack held against the lid","A rigid protective shield"],"answer":4,"exp":"Correct. It keeps pressure off the globe until repair. — CMS I Ocular Trauma - Shah Fallsv.pptx, Slide 17"},{"q":"Which feature makes computed tomography without contrast automatic after trauma?","choices":["Loss of consciousness","Subconjunctival haemorrhage alone","Photophobia","Excessive tearing","A foreign body sensation"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct. It sits alongside alcohol, confusion, tachypnoea, apnoeic breathing, anticoagulants and eye penetration. — CMS I Ocular Trauma - Shah Fallsv.pptx, Slide 6"},{"q":"Which set of symptoms fits a retinal detachment?","choices":["A curtain, floaters, flashes and a field defect","A painful red eye with a hazy cornea and haloes","Gradual painless loss of the peripheral field over years","Pain on eye movement with loss of colour vision","Bilateral disc swelling with transient obscurations"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct. Cloudy or smoky vision belongs there too. — CMS I Ocular Trauma - Shah Fallsv.pptx, Slide 36"},{"q":"A child has severe pain, bradycardia and vomiting on attempting to look up, but the eye looks quiet. What is this?","choices":["A white-eyed blowout","Simple periorbital contusion","Traumatic iritis","A retrobulbar haemorrhage","Concussion without orbital injury"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct. In children entrapment may come with no orbital soft tissue signs at all. — CMS I Ocular Trauma - Shah Fallsv.pptx, Slide 41"},{"q":"A child with a quiet-looking eye vomits and becomes bradycardic each time he looks up after facial trauma. What does that combination represent?","choices":["Gastroenteritis coinciding with the injury","Concussion alone","Muscle entrapment","Raised intracranial pressure","Vasovagal response to pain"],"answer":2,"exp":"Correct. Severe pain, bradycardia and vomiting on eye movement point to entrapment. — CMS I Ocular Trauma - Shah Fallsv.pptx, Slide 41"},{"q":"A foreign body sits in the posterior segment at initial evaluation. What is done?","choices":["Leave it alone for now","Remove it immediately with forceps","Remove it under topical anaesthetic","Irrigate it out with balanced salt solution","Apply a magnet to draw it forward"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct. Retrieving it early risks more damage to ocular structures. — CMS I Ocular Trauma - Shah Fallsv.pptx, Slide 16"},{"q":"A patient has diplopia looking UP after a blow to the orbit. Which muscle is entrapped?","choices":["Superior oblique","Lateral rectus","Levator palpebrae","Inferior rectus","Medial rectus"],"answer":3,"exp":"Correct. Upward gaze diplopia means the inferior rectus or its supporting structures. — CMS I Ocular Trauma - Shah Fallsv.pptx, Slide 41"},{"q":"Ocular trauma is the leading cause of monocular blindness in which group?","choices":["Adolescent girls","Men over seventy","Young adult men","Elderly women","Children under five"],"answer":2,"exp":"Correct. That is the group in which it leads. — CMS I Ocular Trauma - Shah Fallsv.pptx, Slide 5"},{"q":"A laceration has cut the lens capsule. What happens to the lens?","choices":["It is unaffected if the cut is peripheral","It dislocates posteriorly in every case","It calcifies over several days","It becomes hydrated, oedematous and opaque","It hardens and darkens"],"answer":3,"exp":"Correct. Aqueous entering the capsule clouds it. — CMS I Ocular Trauma - Shah Fallsv.pptx, Slide 16"},{"q":"A laceration sits 5 millimetres from the medial canthus. Why does that location matter?","choices":["It cannot be closed surgically","It indicates a basilar skull fracture","The canalicular system may be transected","The optic nerve runs immediately beneath","It always indicates a globe rupture"],"answer":2,"exp":"Correct. If it is not repaired properly the tearing is permanent. — CMS I Ocular Trauma - Shah Fallsv.pptx, Slide 26"},{"q":"A patient who was grinding metal has vertical linear scratches across the cornea. What does that indicate?","choices":["Evert the upper lid to find the object","Examine the lower fornix first","Order an orbital radiograph","Refer immediately for surgical exploration","Patch the eye and review in a week"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct. Vertical linear defects mean it is in the tarsal conjunctiva of the upper lid. — CMS I Ocular Trauma - Shah Fallsv.pptx, Slide 21"},{"q":"How is a periorbital haematoma decompressed?","choices":["Intravenous mannitol alone","Observation until it resorbs","Canthotomy with cantholysis","Needle aspiration through the lid","Pressure patching for 24 hours"],"answer":2,"exp":"Correct. Release the lateral canthal tendon and cut its inferior branch so blood can drain. — CMS I Ocular Trauma - Shah Fallsv.pptx, Slide 33"},{"q":"What distinguishes an orbital contusion from a periorbital haematoma?","choices":["Contusion involves the globe; the haematoma does not","Contusion is bilateral; the haematoma is unilateral","Contusion follows sharp trauma; the haematoma follows blunt","Contusion needs surgery; the haematoma does not","Contusion has no haemorrhage; the haematoma bleeds in the orbit"],"answer":4,"exp":"Correct. The septum is what keeps the contusion in front of the eye. — CMS I Ocular Trauma - Shah Fallsv.pptx, Slide 31"},{"q":"Why are antiemetics given alongside analgesia in a suspected open globe?","choices":["They shorten the time to surgical repair","Vomiting raises intraocular pressure","They reduce the risk of endophthalmitis","They lower the intraocular pressure directly","They allow the eye to be dilated safely"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct. Keeping the patient from straining protects the eye. — CMS I Ocular Trauma - Shah Fallsv.pptx, Slide 17"},{"q":"Which three factors bring a rhegmatogenous detachment forward?","choices":["Sickle cell disease, burns and transfusion","Myopia, cataract surgery and ocular trauma","Hyperopia, glaucoma and dry eye","Diabetes, hypertension and smoking","Contact lens wear, allergy and blepharitis"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct. It is usually age-related, but these accelerate it. — CMS I Ocular Trauma - Shah Fallsv.pptx, Slide 35"},{"q":"What is the aim of hyphema management?","choices":["Reducing photophobia","Preventing a rebleed","Clearing the blood as fast as possible","Restoring accommodation","Preventing infection"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct. Every element of the regimen serves that. — CMS I Ocular Trauma - Shah Fallsv.pptx, Slide 25"},{"q":"Clear fluid drips from a patient's nose after head trauma and leaves a ring within a ring on the bedsheet. What is this?","choices":["A cerebrospinal fluid leak","Simple epistaxis","Lacrimal duct injury","Purulent sinus drainage","Cerebral oedema"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct. Inner ring of blood, outer ring of cerebrospinal fluid. — CMS I Ocular Trauma - Shah Fallsv.pptx, Slide 44"},{"q":"Which action must be avoided whenever ocular trauma is suspected?","choices":["Asking about tetanus status","Inspecting the periorbital tissues","Testing confrontation fields","Dilating the eye","Checking visual acuity"],"answer":3,"exp":"Correct. It removes the pupil examination, one of the few windows onto the inside of the eye. — CMS I Ocular Trauma - Shah Fallsv.pptx, Slide 6"},{"q":"Which feature of orbital floor fracture is specific to children?","choices":["Entrapment never occurs before adolescence","Children always have marked periorbital swelling","Children do not develop infraorbital numbness","Children need no imaging","Entrapment with no soft tissue signs"],"answer":4,"exp":"Correct — the white-eyed blowout. — CMS I Ocular Trauma - Shah Fallsv.pptx, Slide 41"},{"q":"Which drugs should be avoided in a patient with hyphema?","choices":["Cycloplegic drops","Topical corticosteroids","Acetazolamide","Aspirin and antiplatelets","Antiemetics"],"answer":3,"exp":"Correct. They raise the risk of a secondary haemorrhage. — CMS I Ocular Trauma - Shah Fallsv.pptx, Slide 25"},{"q":"Why is surgery for an orbital fracture often delayed one to two weeks?","choices":["To arrange outpatient anaesthetic review","To let orbital swelling settle first","To allow the fracture to begin healing","To complete a course of antibiotics","To wait for the diplopia to resolve spontaneously"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct. Operating into a swollen orbit raises the pressure further. — CMS I Ocular Trauma - Shah Fallsv.pptx, Slide 43"},{"q":"Which type of retinal detachment is NOT treated primarily with surgery?","choices":["All three are surgical","None of the three is surgical","Exudative","Rhegmatogenous","Traction"],"answer":2,"exp":"Correct. With no break and no traction, treatment is directed at the underlying condition. — CMS I Ocular Trauma - Shah Fallsv.pptx, Slide 39"},{"q":"Which bedside test may be positive when cerebrospinal fluid is present?","choices":["A pregnancy test","A dextrose stick","A urine dipstick for protein","Fluorescein staining","A rapid streptococcal antigen test"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct. Cerebrospinal fluid contains glucose. — CMS I Ocular Trauma - Shah Fallsv.pptx, Slide 44"},{"q":"At which sites does a globe most often rupture under blunt force?","choices":["The macula and optic disc","The lacrimal sac","Posterior to the muscle insertions, old incisions, the lamina cribrosa","The central cornea and the visual axis, where the wall is thinnest","The limbus alone"],"answer":2,"exp":"Correct. Those are the weak points. — CMS I Ocular Trauma - Shah Fallsv.pptx, Slide 17"},{"q":"A metal fragment may be lodged in the eye after grinding. Which study is ordered?","choices":["Magnetic resonance imaging of the orbit","Ocular ultrasound with pressure on the globe","Plain orbital radiograph alone","No imaging unless vision is reduced","Computed tomography of the orbit"],"answer":4,"exp":"Correct. It answers the question without moving the fragment. — CMS I Ocular Trauma - Shah Fallsv.pptx, Slide 6"},{"q":"Which condition raises the risk of complications from hyphema?","choices":["Seasonal allergic rhinitis","Hypothyroidism","Migraine with aura","Iron deficiency anaemia","Sickle cell disease"],"answer":4,"exp":"Correct. It is specifically flagged as raising the risk. — CMS I Ocular Trauma - Shah Fallsv.pptx, Slide 25"},{"q":"Why are antibiotics for a cerebrospinal fluid leak controversial?","choices":["They worsen the leak itself","They interfere with computed tomography","They are contraindicated with a skull fracture","They mask the halo sign","They risk selecting resistant organisms"],"answer":4,"exp":"Correct. That is the stated concern. — CMS I Ocular Trauma - Shah Fallsv.pptx, Slide 47"},{"q":"A nail is protruding from a patient's eye. What is done with it?","choices":["Remove it only if the patient is stable","Cut it flush with the cornea","Remove it under topical anaesthetic","Leave it in place","Remove it and irrigate the tract"],"answer":3,"exp":"Correct. It may be tamponading the wound, and removal can extrude intraocular contents. — CMS I Ocular Trauma - Shah Fallsv.pptx, Slide 6"},{"q":"Muscle entrapment is confirmed on computed tomography. Who is called, and why?","choices":["Neurosurgery for admission","No consult; ice and analgesia suffice","Plastic surgery at the next elective list","A facial trauma surgeon urgently","Ophthalmology within 2 to 3 days"],"answer":3,"exp":"Correct. A compromised blood supply is the reason for the urgency. — CMS I Ocular Trauma - Shah Fallsv.pptx, Slide 43"}]};
